  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1057 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nCold Tonight into Saturday Morning...A prevailing high pressure \nsystem has brought cooler Canadian air over the forecast area. The \ncombination of the colder air, clear skies, and calm winds will lead \nto chilly overnight/morning low temperatures ranging from the mid to \nupper 20s for most locations north and west of the Fox Valley, with \nlow to mid 30s in the Fox Valley to the lakeshore. A Freeze Warning \nremains in effect for central, east-central, and portions of far \nnortheast WI, and a Frost Advisory in effect for Door County, where \nthe interior and southern sections of the county will see frost. The \nFreeze Warning and Frost Advisory will remain in effect until 9 AM \nSaturday.\n\nDry and Breezy this Weekend...The forecast area will remain under \nthe influence of surface high pressure and weak mid-level ridging \nfor the weekend. Return southwesterly flow will also be present this \nweekend with a tightened pressure gradient at times. This will lead \nto breezy conditions, mainly during the afternoons with gusts to 20-\n30 mph. Conditions will also be dry and mostly sunny with \ntemperatures rebounding a little, highs in the 50s for Saturday, and \nlow 60s on Sunday.\n\nNext Week...High pressure and mid-level ridging will remain in place \nfor the first half of the work week, keeping conditions dry. \nAlthough chances continue to decrease, guidance indicates the next \nchance for precip would be sometime midweek from the southern tip of \na northern stream system. Mid-level ridging and high pressure then \nreturn for the remainder of the week. This pattern will cause \ntemperatures to remain above normal and gradually warm each day by a \ncouple degrees, potentially reaching the low 70s for some spots by \nThursday of next week. With the return to above normal temperatures \nand a prolonged dry period, will need to monitor fuels across \ndrought stricken areas of northern WI for the potential of an \nincreased wildfire risk.",
